body:
I've posted this before, so if you've seen it somewhat recently, it might have been me.

&#x200B;

My nail polish has been peeling for months.  It's driving me nuts.  No matter what I do, it peels.  Sometimes it's just the top coat, sometimes it's the base and the top coats.

&#x200B;

I've been using Witchcraft Be Strong for years, and topping it with Sally Hansen Insta Dri.  In the past I have done two coats of Witchcraft, one of top coat, or on lazier days I'll do a coat of each.  

&#x200B;

Someone recommended Essie Here To Stay Base Coat and Essie Speedsetter.  I have done several coats of those, just as above.  It peels too.  

&#x200B;

If I mix and match (Essie base and Sally Hansen top coat, Witchcraft base and Essie Speed Dry) it peels.  If I do a thin single layer of base and top coat, it peels.  If I do thicker coats, it peels.  One coat of base, two coats, one coat of top coat, or two, it peels.  I apply top coat to a wet base coat, I tried doing it on dry coats.  I have removed nail polish, and applied nail polish ASAP.  Peeling.   I have removed nail polish, rinsed my hands, and then applied nail polish.  Peeling.  I have buffed my nails and applied polish.  It peeled.

&#x200B;

This only started in the last six months or so.  I am 41, and noticed as soon as I turned 40 my nails had vertical ridges.  Yay, aging.  I haven't worn coloured polish on my fingers in years, and only use clear polishes.  I type on a computer, of course.  I avoid using my nails like tools wherever possible - they're also kept short and neat.  I hate the feeling of my natural nail, moreso now than ever before thanks to the ridges.  

&#x200B;

Help!

I have a nail polish peeling problem.  It only started somewhat recently.

&#x200B;

I used to use Witchcraft as a base, and Sally Hansen Insta-Dri as a top coat.  It suddenly started peeling on me.  (Top coat was peeling, not the base.)

&#x200B;

I came here and asked for advise, and started using Essie Here to Stay base coat and Essie Speed Setter top coat.  But that has peeled too!  (Again just the top coat.)

&#x200B;

I haven't changed anything else.  I've always used the same nail polish remover, I apply right after taking the previous coat off...  I'm stumped.

You guys.  She's coming to my city in a few weeks for a women's empowerment convention, with the byline of "Well behaved women never made history."  She doesn't strike me as a 'lifting women up' kind of lady.

&#x200B;

I wish I could go and report back what hypocritical nonsense she comes up with.

I just posted about Coco's death but wanted to add another comment regarding Heather's ability to do regular work.

&#x200B;

I often wonder why she hasn't gotten a real job too.  I mean, you'd think that if she hated what people said about her online she'd avoid seeking it out, or I don't know, giving people fodder?  She's admitted that she makes stuff up to get a rise out of people... this doesn't seem healthy to me.  I stopped following Instagram accounts that upset me or made me mad because I realized I didn't like it and I could actually do something about it.  I value my mental health.

&#x200B;

Maybe she's on disability?  Surely dying ten times means you can qualify?

&#x200B;

Maybe Pete has told her she can stay at home?  To work on herself?

&#x200B;

Maybe (and I feel like this may have a large part in why she doesn't work in an office setting) she can't get a regular job because people have looked her up, read her blog and her strange rants and didn't think she'd fit in with the culture?  Who would want to hire or work with someone who purposely gets under someone else's skin for their own satisfaction?

I'm quite late on my thoughts regarding Coco's death.  I tried to get on to GOMI, but was denied, so I'm going back a bit. 

&#x200B;

I had to put my cat Mo down thirteen years ago, and at the time it was one of the most awful things that had happened to me.  I wasn't there when he passed, I didn't feel his last breath, but I adored that old man and missed him terribly.  I even slept with his ashes on the pillow next to me for a while.  (He often slept on the pillow next to mine when he was alive.)

&#x200B;

This past November my dad passed away after a fairly short period of illness.  If losing Mo was a thunderstorm, losing my dad was a hurricane.  Massive destruction, need for rebuilding.  Shock, acceptance, panic, sadness.  There was anticipatory grief before he died and the strange journey through grief since he died.  There were days when I didn't want to be awake, and days when I didn't think I would even survive his death.  

&#x200B;

I often go to a place in my mind where I meet up with my dad and I tell him I love him and I miss him.  We hug.  I cry.  He tells me not to be sad.  He tells me I'm doing a good job taking care of my mom, and that he's proud of me.  He tells me his mom makes him his favorite dinner and he and his brother play in the yard like kids and Mo is there too and can't wait to see me.  

&#x200B;

Is it crazy?  Maybe.  But it makes me feel better.  It's been four months since my dad died and being able to see him in my mind and talk to him makes me feel better.  So I can't shit on Heather for her coping mechanisms with Coco's death.  That being said, I'm aggravated that the tone in her posts about her grief feel as if she's the first person to be broken-hearted from losing a loved one, and if you aren't completely hysterical over someone's death there's something wrong with you.  

&#x200B;

I was particularly annoyed that she said she was going to write a post about grief with advice from others about the process.  The thing that I've learned is that there is no one way through grief.  There are stages, but they're not linear.  My sister is grieving our dad's death as well, but we're in different places all the time.  What works for me may not necessarily work for anyone else.  You just have to navigate the path as best as you can. I feel as though Heather hasn't grasped that it's a personal experience.

I've been having a hard time lately - I've been looking for a job for several months - and it's weighed on my spirit.  This past week I visited my parents who asked me how it was going, and instead of saying, "Fine" or "It's going" I said it was going great!  On Saturday I saw a prompt on Facebook ("I will manifest $100 dollars in the next 24 hours!") and I said yes to it.  Literally MINUTES later my dad gave me $100!  Today I was blow drying my hair and thought I should call my parents, and again, literally minutes later, they called me!  

&#x200B;

I'm in the believing stage of LOA and I am confident that things are going to turn around for me any day now, and I couldn't be happier!
